Recolour the icons of the browser from the colour scheme

The chrome of the browser paints its own icons through fill properties.
A toolbar button reads one such property, and the badge of that button
reads a second one. The sheet set the first property alone.
tokens-platform.css of omni.ja gives the badge the accent colour of the
desktop, and toolbar.css gives it a fixed blue under a theme add-on. The
badge of a user of QtCurve therefore took no colour of the scheme.

The map now binds the two attention properties to the selection rows.
One row moves the sheet and the manifest alike, and the static arm
already held the icons_attention key of that row.

Three further properties hold the text colour of the element that reads
them, which the map gives each surface. tokens-shared.css declares the
two button properties on the root, and menu.css declares the menu
property on the popup. A rule of the root cannot reach a property of the
popup, so the sheet carries a second block. A custom property crosses a
shadow boundary, so the button property reaches the icon of the tab
audio button inside its shadow tree.

No rule names context-fill, context-stroke or the preference that gates
them. The browser allows the context paint of a chrome image alone, so a
glyph of an inline image of the sheet paints black. The theme asks a
user for no preference beside the one of the sheets.

assert-ff-icons.sh reads the sheet of a true gate and the profile of a
false gate. It reads the three pinned properties, the two attention
values against their rows, and every keyword above. Five doctored copies
must fail it.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 8 of 13. The branch holds those thirteen commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme. Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve
holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

